::PC_U05_L08_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U05_L08_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1; MLA.PC.VC.2; MLA.PC.VC.3
Question:
Which statement best summarizes complex-number work on the complex plane?
{
~A. Only imaginary parts are graphed.#The real part is also needed.
=B. Complex numbers can be represented, measured, and interpreted using coordinate-plane ideas.#Correct. Complex numbers connect algebraic form to coordinate-plane representation, distance, midpoint, and interpretation.
~C. Complex numbers cannot model movement.#Complex-plane movement can be modeled.
~D. Distance and midpoint do not apply to complex numbers.#Distance and midpoint apply when a + bi is treated as (a, b).
}
::PC_U05_L08_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U05_L08_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.4; MLA.PC.VC.5
Question:
Why are polar and trigonometric forms useful for complex numbers?
{
~A. They remove angles.#Angles are part of polar and trigonometric form.
~B. They ignore distance from the origin.#Distance is represented by the modulus.
~C. They make every complex number real.#Complex numbers do not automatically become real.
=D. They show distance and direction, which supports multiplication in trigonometric form.#Correct. The modulus gives distance and the argument gives direction.
}
::PC_U05_L08_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U05_L08_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.1; MLA.PC.PA.2; MLA.PC.PA.3
Question:
Which conic is defined by a center and constant radius?
{
=A. Circle#Correct. A circle is the set of points a fixed distance from a center.
~B. Parabola#A parabola is defined by a focus and directrix.
~C. Ellipse#An ellipse uses two foci and a constant sum of distances.
~D. Hyperbola#A hyperbola uses two foci and a constant difference of distances.
}
::PC_U05_L08_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U05_L08_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.4; MLA.PC.PA.5; MLA.PC.PA.6; MLA.PC.PA.7
Question:
Which comparison is correct?
{
~A. Parabolas and ellipses are both defined by one radius.#That describes a circle, not parabolas and ellipses.
~B. Ellipses have no center.#Ellipses do have a center.
=C. Parabolas use a vertex, while ellipses use a center and axes.#Correct. These key features distinguish the two conics.
~D. Parabolas always have two branches.#Hyperbolas have two branches; parabolas have one connected curve.
}
::PC_U05_L08_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U05_L08_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.8; MLA.PC.PA.9
Question:
Which feature is especially important when sketching hyperbolas?
{
~A. A single radius#Radius belongs to circles.
=B. Asymptotes#Correct. Asymptotes guide hyperbola branches.
~C. One endpoint only#Hyperbolas are not determined by one endpoint only.
~D. The imaginary axis#The imaginary axis is a complex-plane feature, not a hyperbola feature.
}